Label a decoded frame with the size that was actually written

VideoFrame.allocationSize() and copyTo() default their `rect` to the frame's
VISIBLE rect, so the ArrayBuffer holds the visible picture. The dual-plane
buffer built from it is then labelled `codedSize`, taken straight off the
VideoFrame. When the two differ, every consumer slices the planes from a label
that does not describe the bytes.

Measured with x264 through compositor-proxy on a real xterm:

    coded   1024 x 770
    visible 1024 x 768      <- what copyTo wrote

convertYUVAArrayBufferInto then computes lumaSize = 1024*770 = 788480 against a
buffer holding only 786432 bytes of luma, so:

  * the Y slice eats the first 2048 bytes of the U plane;
  * U and V are both read 2048 bytes (4 chroma rows) late;
  * V runs 3072 bytes past the end, where subarray() silently clamps and the
    tail of the chroma texture is never written.

Displaced chroma renders as a diagonal; never-written chroma renders as flat
cyan under it.

The fix is to label the buffer with the visible size, because that is what was
copied. It is also the size worth showing -- the extra coded rows are padding,
not picture -- and it keeps the plane arithmetic self-consistent, since that
code slices chroma with `>> 1` and `>> 2` and only agrees with itself on even
dimensions.

--- a/src/remote/webcodec-buffer-decoder.ts
+++ b/src/remote/webcodec-buffer-decoder.ts
@@ onComplete
                 opaque: {
                     buffer: new Uint8Array(opaqueBuffer),
                     codedSize: {
-                        width: opaqueVideoFrame.codedWidth,
-                        height: opaqueVideoFrame.codedHeight,
+                        width: opaqueVideoFrame.visibleRect?.width ?? opaqueVideoFrame.codedWidth,
+                        height: opaqueVideoFrame.visibleRect?.height ?? opaqueVideoFrame.codedHeight,
                     },
                 },
@@ onComplete (alpha)
                 dualPlaneYUVABuffer.alpha = {
                     buffer: new Uint8Array(alphaBuffer),
                     codedSize: {
-                        width: decodeResult.alpha.buffer.codedWidth,
-                        height: decodeResult.alpha.buffer.codedHeight,
+                        width: decodeResult.alpha.buffer.visibleRect?.width ?? decodeResult.alpha.buffer.codedWidth,
+                        height: decodeResult.alpha.buffer.visibleRect?.height ?? decodeResult.alpha.buffer.codedHeight,
                     },
                 }
@@ opaqueOutput / alphaOutput
-        const width = buffer.codedWidth;
-        const height = buffer.codedHeight;
+        const width = buffer.visibleRect?.width ?? buffer.codedWidth;
+        const height = buffer.visibleRect?.height ?? buffer.codedHeight;

NOTE: opaqueOutput/alphaOutput record a codedSize that the I420 path never
reads -- onComplete rebuilds its own. Both sites are corrected here so the two
paths cannot disagree, but the I420 one is the one that changes behaviour.
